---
import Plate from '../../plate/Plate.astro';
import FilmControls from '../../plate/FilmControls.astro';
import FilmSubtitles from '../../plate/FilmSubtitles.astro';
import FilmTranscript from '../../plate/FilmTranscript.astro';
import '../../plate/film.css';
import { renderFormula } from '../../../lib/formulas/render';
import { createWarmModel, contours, extrema, type Point } from './math';
import { stippleSample } from './drawing';
import { CUES, TIMELINE } from './story';
import './style.css';

const model = createWarmModel();
const mode = extrema(model.phi1);
const hot = model.cells[mode.hot];
const cold = model.cells[mode.cold];

if (!hot || !cold || !(mode.spread > 0)) throw new Error('The computed first mode has distinct hot and cold cells.');

const project = (point: Point): Point => ({ x: 250 + point.x * 145, y: 178 - point.y * 145 });
const boundary = model.boundary.map(point => {
  const at = project(point);

  return `${at.x},${at.y}`;
}).join(' ');
const insulatedEdge = model.boundary.map(point => {
  const at = project({ x: point.x * 1.035, y: point.y * 1.035 });

  return `${at.x},${at.y}`;
}).join(' ');
const levels = Array.from({ length: 7 }, (_, index) => mode.min + (index + 1) * mode.spread / 8);
const segments = contours(model, model.phi1, levels).map(segment => ({ a: project(segment.a), b: project(segment.b) }));
const dots: Point[] = [];

for (const [index, point] of model.cells.entries()) {
  const value = model.phi1[index];

  if (value === undefined) throw new Error('Every stippled cell has its computed first-mode value.');

  const density = (value - mode.min) / mode.spread;

  for (let slot = 0; slot < 2; slot++) {
    const sample = stippleSample(index, slot);

    if (sample.threshold < .01 + density * .66) dots.push(project({ x: point.x + sample.x * model.h, y: point.y + sample.y * model.h }));
  }
}

const hotRing = project(hot);
const coldRing = project(cold);
const mu = renderFormula(String.raw`\mu_1 = ${model.mu1.toFixed(2)}`, false);
---

<Plate
  caption="A plate warmed in one place and cooled in another evens out behind an insulated edge. Its hottest and coldest points drift to the edge and stay there, at the ends of the shape that is left as the heat fades. The paper claims this for every smooth plate without holes, with no flat point inside at all."
  leftOut="the proof's machinery; multiple eigenvalues; the three-dimensional counterexample of Atar and Burdzy; the exact Burdzy–Werner domain. The finite-grid calculation illustrates one plate and does not verify the theorem for all smooth plates without holes."
>
  <uruk-warm-spot class="film-host">
    <div class="wsfilm">
      <div class="wsfilm-stage" data-film-viewport>
        <canvas class="wsfilm-canvas" data-film-canvas role="img" aria-label="Ink contours and stippled warmth evolve on an insulated plate. A plus ring marks the hottest grid cell and a minus ring the coldest. The field settles towards the computed first Neumann mode. Gradient arrows show its nonzero sampled interior slopes. A schematic plate with holes recalls the known counterexamples, and a blue boundary band marks the unreviewed general claim. A full text transcript follows."></canvas>
        <span class="wsfilm-label" data-label="mode-mu"><span set:html={mu} /></span>
        <div class="wsfilm-still" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 500 380" role="presentation">
            <defs><clipPath id="wsfilm-still-plate"><polygon points={boundary} /></clipPath></defs>
            <text x="250" y="25" text-anchor="middle" font-size="23">THIS PLATE'S FIRST NEUMANN MODE</text>
            <g clip-path="url(#wsfilm-still-plate)">
              {dots.map(point => <circle cx={point.x} cy={point.y} r=".9" fill="var(--ink)" opacity=".4" />)}
              {segments.map(segment => <line x1={segment.a.x} y1={segment.a.y} x2={segment.b.x} y2={segment.b.y} stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.2" />)}
            </g>
            <polygon points={insulatedEdge} fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width=".9" />
            <polygon points={boundary} fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.7" />
            <circle cx={hotRing.x} cy={hotRing.y} r="8" fill="var(--paper)" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2" />
            <circle cx={coldRing.x} cy={coldRing.y} r="8" fill="var(--paper)" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2" />
            <text class="wsfilm-still-math" x={hotRing.x} y={hotRing.y + 5.5} text-anchor="middle" font-size="19">+</text>
            <text class="wsfilm-still-math" x={coldRing.x} y={coldRing.y + 5.5} text-anchor="middle" font-size="19">−</text>
            <text class="wsfilm-still-math" x="250" y="336" text-anchor="middle" font-size="25">μ₁ = {model.mu1.toFixed(2)}</text>
            <text x="250" y="365" text-anchor="middle" font-size="21">BOTH EXTREMES IN EDGE CELLS · 64 × 64 GRID</text>
          </svg>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="wsfilm-fallback-caption">The computed first Neumann mode on this 64 × 64 grid has its warmest and coldest cells at the edge. The plus and minus rings mark those cells. Contrast is rescaled to show the final mode. This is one computed plate, and the no-JavaScript still does not run the heat flow.</p>
      <FilmSubtitles cues={CUES} />
    </div>
    <FilmControls id="wsfilm-timeline" timeline={TIMELINE} step={50} />
    <FilmTranscript cues={CUES}>
      <p class="film-method">How the film is made. The plate is the radial outline r = 1 + 0.28 cos 2θ + 0.12 sin 3θ. Its computation uses a 64 × 64 grid over the square from −1.5 to 1.5 in each direction, with 1,444 cells inside the outline. A five-point Neumann stencil has no flux across the grid edge. The double outline is the insulation symbol. It does not add another region to the calculation.</p>
      <p class="film-method">The initial field is one warm Gaussian blob and one cold Gaussian blob. A worker computes the full finite-grid heat equation using a converged matrix-exponential calculation, preserving every grid degree of freedom. The playback interpolates temperature vectors between computed times. It recomputes the displayed field's extrema and spread from those same vectors. The plus and minus rings mark the actual hottest and coldest cell centres, and can jump between cells when the maximizer changes. An edge cell has a missing stencil neighbour; its centre need not lie exactly on the smooth drawn outline.</p>
      <p class="film-method">While the heat calculation is pending, the film identifies the genuine initial field and withholds heat-time and spread readouts. A failed worker leaves an explicit failure message. The first-mode data are shipped independently and remain available as a computed still. No completed heat readouts are substituted while the calculation is unavailable.</p>
      <p class="film-method">Seven contour levels rescale with the field. The spread readout is the actual hottest-minus-coldest temperature difference. A fixed stipple mask becomes denser in warmer cells, and its identities remain fixed as the field changes. The contrast is rescaled to make the limiting first mode visible. Contours use complete interior grid quadrilaterals, leaving a thin unplotted strip beside the edge. This rendering choice does not alter the cell values or extrema.</p>
      <p class="film-method">The first positive grid eigenvalue is approximately {model.mu1.toFixed(4)}, with the next approximately {model.mu2.toFixed(3)}. The shipped eigenvector is checked against the same stencil. Gradient arrows show directions of the final mode, with lengths normalized for visibility. The magnitude ratio is a separate calculation on sampled cells more than three grid spacings from the grid edge. The minimum magnitude on that subset is {model.gradientRatio.toFixed(3)} of its maximum. This finite sample does not establish nonvanishing gradient at every interior point of the smooth plate.</p>
      <p class="film-method">The plate-with-holes inset is schematic. It recalls the published counterexamples and has no simulated counterexample field. Blue marks the claim for every smooth plate without holes. Burdzy's later use of the claimed theorem does not assess its proof. The closing evidence follows the article's date, 17 October 2026, and keeps the label unreviewed. One numerical plate is not a verification of the general theorem.</p>
    </FilmTranscript>
  </uruk-warm-spot>
</Plate>

<script>
  import './element';
</script>
